// Choose Your Codec — the learning ladder.
// CYC.TUTOR is the tutor: short explanations of what to do next and why, in compression terms.
// Levels move from coarse (what the stages are, why entropy is the floor) to bit-level (block layouts, ANS state).
// Lessons may embed an algorithm's lab via `demo` and point at a duel via `duel` (index into CYC.DUELS).
// Formulas are plain text/HTML (sup/sub), no LaTeX library.
// A tutor line that mentions an algorithm family ends with a link `[Read: <title>](map/zoo/<slug>/)`; the UI renders it.

window.CYC = window.CYC || {};

CYC.TUTOR = {
  name: 'Tutor',
  portrait: 'img/fighters/none.png',
  // Trigger keys the UI fires: level_intro:<lvl>, heist_intro:<taskId>, chair_open:<role>, pick:<algorithmId>,
  // before_run, result:<rank>, level_complete:<lvl>, explore_intro, duel_intro, matrix_intro. One-shot unless `always`.
  lines: {
    'level_intro:1': [
      'Level 1 is lossless coding with two stages. Prediction guesses each symbol from what is already decoded and emits only the miss. Entropy coding turns the stream of misses into bits. Pick one algorithm for each stage, then run the pipeline.',
      'The floor is entropy. A symbol with probability p costs −log<sub>2</sub> p bits under any lossless code, and no coder beats the average of that price, H. Bits are saved by making symbols predictable, not by packing them more cleverly. [Read: Modeling and coding](map/zoo/modeling-and-coding/)',
    ],
    'level_intro:2': [
      'Level 2 is lossy coding. One new stage may discard information: Quantization, which rounds values onto a grid or a codebook. It is the only irreversible stage; every other stage is still exact bookkeeping. [Read: Rate and distortion](map/zoo/rate-and-distortion/)',
      'Two stages set the quantizer up. A Transform re-expresses the data so that most coefficients are near zero and round to zero at almost no error. A Perceptual model changes the error measure so that error the eye or ear cannot detect counts as zero. Place both before you choose a step size.',
    ],
    'level_intro:3': [
      'Level 3 adds the Shared prior: what the decoder holds before the first byte arrives. Standard tables, a dictionary trained on your data, a VQ codebook, a decoder network. The more the two ends agree on in advance, the fewer bits travel. [Read: Side information](map/zoo/side-information/)',
      'The cost of a prior is not in the stream. It is size, versioning, and a decoder that only understands one kind of data. Watch the side-information meter next to the ratio: a 100 KB dictionary that saves 50 bytes per message pays for itself only after 2,000 messages.',
    ],
    'level_intro:4': [
      'Level 4 is bit-level formats decoded inside GPU kernels and attention loops. Random access and parallel decode matter more than the last 20% of ratio, so the best entropy coder here is often none at all: fixed-length codes, one multiply per element. [Read: Block-scaled formats](map/zoo/block-float-formats/)',
      'You will count bits per weight, (scale bits + n·b) / n, look at block layouts, and see why an orthogonal rotation, which changes no information, halves the error of a 4-bit grid by spreading outliers over all channels.',
    ],
    'level_intro:5': [
      'Level 5 is the KV cache. A 128k-token context of a 70B model is 40 GB of keys and values, re-read on every generated token. The same bytes appear in five tasks: resident in HBM, offloaded to host memory over PCIe, stored cold, streamed over a network, or evicted token by token. A sixth option is to design the model to store less. Each task fills different stages. [Read: The KV cache, five tasks](map/zoo/kv-cache-vaults/)',
      'Two facts drive every pick on this level. Keys are not values: keys have a few outlier channels at 20× magnitude and are low-rank before RoPE; values are neither. And the consumer of the data is the softmax, which weights about 1% of tokens heavily and the rest near zero.',
    ],
    'heist_intro:onebit': ['Same tensor as the KV cache resident in HBM, but at 1–2 bits per channel. A scalar quantizer has two or four levels per channel and runs out of precision. Channels are correlated, so quantize groups of channels together with a codebook: the codebook pays the joint entropy, not the sum of the marginals. Undo RoPE first, because RoPE smears each outlier across two channels per position. [Read: Vector quantization](map/zoo/vector-quantization/)'],
    'heist_intro:overflow': ['Each decode step gathers about 2,000 tokens out of 128k, scattered across host memory, over a PCIe link shared with every other request on the GPU. Keep a small proxy index on the GPU to choose tokens, a GPU-resident buffer about twice the size of the pick list, and the lossless original one hop away in host memory. No variable-length codes: every token must be fetched by address. [Read: Attention as the audience](map/zoo/attention-as-audience/)'],
    'heist_intro:coldkv': ['Cold storage is the one KV task where the whole prefix is available before encoding, it is encoded once, and nothing attends over the stored form. Random access is gone, so an entropy coder is useful again: transform, quantize, then entropy-code, as JPEG does. The limit is decode time: decompressing must stay well under the time to recompute the prefix. [Read: The KV cache, five tasks](map/zoo/kv-cache-vaults/)'],
    'heist_intro:kvcourier': ['The channel is a network, so every byte is latency, and an entropy coder with a real probability model per layer and channel pays off. Predict each token from a fixed anchor token, not from its predecessor: a chain of deltas must be decoded sequentially, while anchor deltas decode in parallel and bound the error drift. [Read: Linear prediction](map/zoo/linear-prediction/)'],
    'heist_intro:purge': ['Token eviction is a quantizer with two levels per token: keep everything or drop everything. The hard stage is the Perceptual model: FlashAttention never writes the attention scores to memory, so token importance must be rebuilt from a proxy. Memory is paged in blocks of 16 tokens, and a block is freed only when all 16 are gone. [Read: Attention as the audience](map/zoo/attention-as-audience/)'],
    'heist_intro:bornsmall': ['This task is decided before pretraining. The largest KV reductions were architectural: grouped-query attention stores 8 KV heads instead of 64; multi-head latent attention stores one 512-dim latent per token instead of two 128×128 tensors. Only the Transform stage applies, and the transform is trained with the model. [Read: Learned transforms](map/zoo/learned-transforms/)'],
    'pick:unrope': ['RoPE is a known rotation per position, so undoing it costs zero bits: the decoder re-applies it. What it buys: outlier channels stay in fixed positions, so per-channel scales work, and the key matrix becomes low-rank again. [Read: Rotations and rank](map/zoo/rotations-and-rank/)'],
    'pick:coupled': ['The joint entropy of eight channels is at most the sum of their eight marginal entropies, and the gap is their mutual information. A codebook over the eight channels pays the joint; eight scalar quantizers pay the sum. [Read: Vector quantization](map/zoo/vector-quantization/)'],
    'pick:spotlight': ['A perceptual model for a transformer reader. Distortion on a token the softmax weights near zero costs nothing, so select the top-k tokens with a cheap proxy and fetch only those. The proxy index is itself a small cache, read in full every step: keep it uncompressed. [Read: Attention as the audience](map/zoo/attention-as-audience/)'],
    'pick:evict': ['Eviction is a two-level quantizer per token: keep or drop. Rate-distortion still applies, with distortion measured in benchmark points. Memory is paged: a 16-token block is freed only when every token in it is gone, so eviction without compaction frees nothing. [Read: Attention as the audience](map/zoo/attention-as-audience/)'],
    'pick:tiered': ['A tiered cache is side information measured in HBM: the lossless copy sits in host memory one PCIe hop away, so the GPU copy may be lossy. It works only if the fetch is hidden behind compute. [Read: Side information](map/zoo/side-information/)'],
    'pick:waterline': ['Reverse water-filling: give bits to the coordinates with variance above a threshold, none to the rest. It needs a transform in front of it; on raw channels every coordinate has similar variance and there is nothing to allocate. [Read: Scalar quantization](map/zoo/scalar-quantization/)'],
    'pick:mla': ['Zero encode cost, zero decode cost, and no choice afterwards: the transform was trained with the model. Everything a codec adds later works on the latent this stage leaves behind. [Read: Learned transforms](map/zoo/learned-transforms/)'],
    'level_complete:5': ['You saw the same 40 GB go through five tasks and needed a different pipeline each time. That is the whole field in one object: the task fixes the requirements, the requirements decide which stages are filled, and the stages decide the algorithms. The bytes never changed.'],
    'heist_intro:archive': ['Read the requirements first. Lossless required is on; Side information allowed is off. So: no Quantization, no data-specific prior. Then ask what structure arbitrary files have. They repeat themselves, so a dictionary matcher is the predictor. [Read: The LZ family](map/zoo/lz-family/)'],
    'heist_intro:pixelart': ['Screenshots have flat regions and repeated tiles. A neighbouring pixel is a good prediction for a pixel, so a spatial predictor turns gradients into zeros; LZ77 then catches the repeated tiles. Two predictors can share the Prediction stage here. [Read: Linear prediction](map/zoo/linear-prediction/)'],
    'heist_intro:studio': ['Audio is a signal, not a string of symbols. Its structure is resonance: each sample is close to a weighted sum of the last few. The residual of a good linear predictor is a narrow spike around zero, a geometric distribution, and Golomb-Rice is the optimal prefix code for exactly that shape. [Read: Prefix codes](map/zoo/prefix-codes/)'],
    'heist_intro:gallery': ['Six stages, and the real codec fills all six. Photographs forgive smooth errors; the eye resolves color at half the resolution of brightness and barely sees fine detail. Transform first, quantize with a frequency-weighted table, then entropy-code the long runs of zeros. [Read: Block transforms](map/zoo/block-transforms/)'],
    'heist_intro:walkman': ['Encoder compute is high; Decoder compute is a 60 MHz chip. Put the expensive analysis in the encoder: a psychoacoustic model that finds where hearing is masked, so the quantizer can cut there. The decoder only reads scale factors and runs an inverse transform. [Read: Perceptual models](map/zoo/perceptual-models/)'],
    'heist_intro:primetime': ['One encode, a billion decodes. Each frame is nearly identical to the previous one, so motion compensation predicts it from a shifted block of the reference frame. Bandwidth pressure is maximal, so a slower entropy coder is justified if the decoder can afford it. [Read: Motion compensation](map/zoo/motion-compensation/)'],
    'heist_intro:library': ['The opposite corner from real-time speech: unlimited time, unlimited memory, a patient decoder. Ratio is the only requirement. Choose the predictor with the best probability model regardless of cost, and a coder that charges exactly −log<sub>2</sub> p. [Read: Context models](map/zoo/context-models/)'],
    'heist_intro:phonecall': ['Latency tolerance is the hard constraint: anything that holds a full frame plus lookahead before emitting is out. And 3 kbps is impossible unless the decoder already knows what speech sounds like, so most of the information must live in the Shared prior. [Read: Learned transforms](map/zoo/learned-transforms/)'],
    'heist_intro:logpipe': ['Three hundred bytes per message. LZ77 alone finds nothing: there is no history inside the message yet. But the schema never changes between messages. A trained dictionary preloads the match window, giving the predictor a memory before the message starts. [Read: Side information](map/zoo/side-information/)'],
    'heist_intro:vectorvault': ['A billion codes are scanned per query, each decoded on its own, so fixed-length codes are mandatory. The compression comes from a codebook: product quantization splits each vector into sub-vectors and codes each one with 8 bits. The codebook lives in the index file. [Read: Vector quantization](map/zoo/vector-quantization/)'],
    'heist_intro:weightsvault': ['The decoder is a matmul kernel: one multiply per element, every thread on its own block. Decoder compute is the minimum on the scale. A block-scaled integer format gives exactly that; then look at the Entropy coding stage and ask whether a variable-length code could run there at all. [Read: Block-scaled formats](map/zoo/block-float-formats/)'],
    'heist_intro:kvcache': ['Same as the LLM weights task, except nothing can be calibrated in advance: tokens arrive one at a time, and the encoder has the same zero budget as the decoder. Keys have a few outlier channels; quantize keys per channel or rotate them first. [Read: Block-scaled formats](map/zoo/block-float-formats/)'],
    'heist_intro:webphoto': ['The Photographs task thirty years later. Same data, but encoder compute is unlimited and bandwidth still costs. Which stages improve when the encoder may search? Spatial prediction of blocks, a fractional-bit entropy coder, a learned transform. [Read: Learned transforms](map/zoo/learned-transforms/)'],
    'chair_open:predict': ['Prediction produces no bits. It outputs a residual, or match tokens: a stream with the same number of symbols but a much narrower histogram. Entropy went down; the entropy coder collects the saving. [Read: Linear prediction](map/zoo/linear-prediction/)'],
    'chair_open:entropy': ['Pick the entropy coder by the histogram it will see. Large alphabet and no dominant symbol: Huffman is within 0.1 bit of H. One symbol above 50%: Huffman spends a full bit on it, so use arithmetic coding or ANS, or run-length-code first. Geometric spike around zero: Golomb-Rice. [Read: Prefix codes](map/zoo/prefix-codes/)'],
    'chair_open:transform': ['A transform removes no information. 64 numbers in, 64 numbers out, same total energy (Parseval). What changes is where the energy sits: if it concentrates in a few coefficients, the quantizer can zero the rest at almost no error. [Read: Block transforms](map/zoo/block-transforms/)'],
    'chair_open:quantize': ['The only irreversible stage. It trades rate for distortion: with step Δ the error variance is Δ²/12, and each doubling of Δ saves about one bit per value. A codebook beats a grid when the dimensions are correlated. [Read: Rate and distortion](map/zoo/rate-and-distortion/)'],
    'chair_open:perceptual': ['A perceptual model never touches the stream. It changes the distortion measure: error where the ear is masked or the eye cannot resolve detail counts as zero. The quantizer then cuts deeper at the same perceived quality. [Read: Perceptual models](map/zoo/perceptual-models/)'],
    'chair_open:prior': ['Everything the decoder holds before the stream arrives. Standard tables cost nothing to ship and save little. A trained dictionary or codebook saves a lot and must be versioned. A decoder network is tens of megabytes of prior. [Read: Side information](map/zoo/side-information/)'],
    'pick:huffman': ['Huffman coding builds the optimal code with integer code lengths. Average length lies between H and H+1 bits per symbol; the gap is largest when one symbol dominates. [Read: Prefix codes](map/zoo/prefix-codes/)'],
    'pick:arith': ['Arithmetic coding pays exactly −log<sub>2</sub> p per symbol, fractions included. Driven by a context model it is the strongest entropy coder, and the slowest. [Read: Arithmetic coding and ANS](map/zoo/arithmetic-and-ans/)'],
    'pick:lz77': ['A back-reference is a prediction that came true: "the next 9 bytes are the ones 14 bytes back". The tokens still need an entropy coder: distances and lengths have their own histograms. [Read: The LZ family](map/zoo/lz-family/)'],
    'pick:blockint': ['4 bits per weight plus a 16-bit scale per 32 weights: 4.5 bits per weight. No entropy coder follows, so that is the final number. [Read: Block-scaled formats](map/zoo/block-float-formats/)'],
    'pick:dictionary': ['A trained dictionary is the lossless cousin of a codebook. Trained once, shipped out of band, referenced by an ID in the frame header. Lose the dictionary and the data is unreadable. [Read: Side information](map/zoo/side-information/)'],
    'pick:rvq': ['Eight codebooks of 1024 entries: 80 bits per frame, 1024<sup>8</sup> effective reconstruction points, 8192 centroids stored. Drop the last codebooks and the stream still decodes, at lower quality. [Read: Vector quantization](map/zoo/vector-quantization/)'],
    'pick:cm': ['Context mixing gives the best ratio of any lossless method. It is also the slowest, and symmetric: the decoder recomputes every probability the encoder used. Nothing real-time runs through it. [Read: Context models](map/zoo/context-models/)'],
    'before_run': ['Before you run, read the size meter. RAW is the input size. BOUND is what an ideal entropy coder would pay for the symbols your pipeline produced. FINAL is what your entropy coder actually paid. The gap between BOUND and FINAL is the coder\'s inefficiency; the gap between RAW and BOUND is the modeling gain.'],
    'result:S': ['Exactly the real codec\'s choices, stage for stage. Read the reveal anyway: the details are where the engineering lives.'],
    'result:A': ['A valid pipeline that differs from the real codec. Compare your picks with the reveal and ask which constraint the original designers had that you did not.'],
    'result:B': ['The task is solved. Read the yellow verdicts: each marks a stage where a property of the algorithm does not match a requirement of the task.'],
    'result:C': ['Read the red verdicts. Each names the requirement and the algorithm property that conflict. That conflict is the lesson; the grade is only the summary.'],
    'result:D': ['Start from the requirements. Lossless required rules out every quantizer and every perceptual model. Latency tolerance rules out anything that buffers a frame. Eliminate before you choose.'],
    'level_complete:1': ['You now know the floor, entropy, and the split: a model that makes symbols predictable and a coder that charges −log<sub>2</sub> p for them. Everything above this level either improves the model or makes the error measure more forgiving.'],
    'level_complete:2': ['Rate-distortion. One irreversible stage, a transform that makes the loss cheap, and a perceptual model that redefines error. JPEG, MP3 and H.264 are the same four moves on different data.'],
    'level_complete:3': ['Side information. The stream is half the story and the prior is the other half: a dictionary, a codebook and a decoder network are the same idea at three sizes. And latency is a constraint that no ratio can buy back.'],
    'level_complete:4': ['Bit level. Fixed-length codes for random access, block scales to contain outliers, rotations that preserve dot products, and an entropy coder that reaches the bound at table-lookup speed. That is the state of the art on your own machine.'],
    'explore_intro': ['No task selected. Browse the algorithms, load the real codecs\' pipelines, run a duel (two algorithms in the same stage on the same data), or study the trade-off map. Everything here runs the same engine as the tasks.'],
    'duel_intro': ['Same stage, same data, two algorithms. The only fair comparison holds the rest of the pipeline fixed and changes one stage. Watch BOUND and FINAL separately: one measures the model, the other the coder.'],
    'matrix_intro': ['Rows are algorithms, columns are requirements a task can impose. A ✘ marks a hard conflict: that algorithm cannot be used in a task with that requirement. This table is the whole game in one picture.'],
  },
};

CYC.LESSONS = {
  1: [
    { id: 'entropy', title: 'Entropy is the floor',
      body: 'A source that emits symbol s with probability p(s) cannot be coded losslessly, on average, below <b>H = −Σ p(s) log<sub>2</sub> p(s)</b> bits per symbol. That is Shannon\'s source coding theorem, and it is a floor, not a target: a coder can get within a fraction of a bit of it, never under it. The only way to lower the floor is to change the probabilities, which means predicting better. A fair coin: H = 1 bit. A 90/10 coin: H ≈ 0.47 bits. English letters, counted one at a time: ≈ 4.1 bits. English with a good model of what came before: ≈ 1 bit per letter.',
      demo: 'huffman', takeaway: 'Bits measure surprise. −log₂ p is the price of one symbol; H is the average price.' },
    { id: 'modelcode', title: 'Modeling vs coding',
      body: 'Every lossless compressor is two halves. The <b>model</b> answers: what is the probability of the next symbol, given what I have seen? The <b>coder</b> turns that probability into bits, paying −log<sub>2</sub> p. All the cleverness is in the model; coders are commodities (Huffman, arithmetic, ANS) and you pick one by speed. The Prediction stage is the model. A prediction residual is modeling: the same count of symbols, with a histogram collapsed around zero. An LZ77 match is modeling: "the next 9 bytes are the ones 14 bytes back", probability ≈ 1, cost ≈ a few bits for the pointer.',
      demo: 'lz77', duel: 2, takeaway: 'Prediction lowers H. Entropy coding pays H. Do not confuse the two.' },
    { id: 'prefix', title: 'Prefix codes and the one-bit tax',
      body: 'A prefix code assigns each symbol a whole number of bits, with no code a prefix of another, so the stream decodes without separators. Kraft\'s inequality says such lengths exist iff <b>Σ 2<sup>−l(s)</sup> ≤ 1</b>. Huffman finds the optimal lengths and lands between H and H+1 bits per symbol; the waste is at most p<sub>max</sub> + 0.086. When one symbol has p > 0.5 (think: zeros after quantization) Huffman still spends a full bit on it while its information content is under 1 bit. Arithmetic coding fixes this by coding the whole message as one number in [0,1): each symbol narrows the interval by a factor p, and the final width 2<sup>−B</sup> costs B bits in total, fractions included.',
      demo: 'arith', duel: 0, takeaway: 'Huffman: fast, within 1 bit. Arithmetic/ANS: exact, fractional bits. Run-length the zeros if you must use Huffman.' },
    { id: 'rice', title: 'Residuals want Golomb-Rice',
      body: 'Subtract a good prediction and the residual follows a two-sided geometric (Laplacian) distribution: lots of 0 and ±1, a long thin tail. For a geometric source the optimal prefix code is a <b>Golomb code</b>; with parameter 2<sup>k</sup> it is the Rice code: write n ≫ k in unary, then the k low bits raw. One parameter per block, no table, no tree, a handful of bit operations. FLAC and JPEG-LS live on this.',
      demo: 'rice', duel: 6, takeaway: 'Match the entropy coder to the histogram shape. Spike around zero → Golomb-Rice.' },
  ],
  2: [
    { id: 'rd', title: 'Lossy means a quantizer, nothing else',
      body: 'Lossy codecs are lossless codecs with exactly one irreversible stage: the quantizer. Everything else is still reversible bookkeeping. Quantization trades <b>rate</b> for <b>distortion</b>: with uniform step Δ the error variance is Δ²/12, and each doubling of Δ saves about 1 bit per value while quadrupling the squared error (≈ 6 dB of SNR per bit). The curve of achievable (rate, distortion) pairs is the rate-distortion function R(D); every lossy codec is a point on it, and better codecs push the curve down-left by quantizing smarter values (after a transform) or with a smarter grid (a codebook).',
      demo: 'uniform', takeaway: 'Find the quantizer and you have found where the loss is. Δ²/12, 6 dB per bit.' },
    { id: 'transform', title: 'Why transform first',
      body: 'Quantizing pixels directly spends the same bits everywhere. An orthogonal transform (DCT, wavelet, MDCT) keeps total energy and total squared error unchanged (Parseval) but concentrates the energy into a few coefficients. Now most coefficients quantize to zero at almost no cost in error, and the few big ones carry the picture. The <b>transform coding gain</b> is the ratio of the arithmetic to the geometric mean of the coefficient variances: 1 for white noise, large for smooth images. Prediction and transforms are two routes to the same goal, decorrelation; video codecs use both.',
      demo: 'dct', duel: 7, takeaway: 'A transform changes where the energy sits, not how much there is. That is enough.' },
    { id: 'perceptual', title: 'The perceptual model changes the error measure',
      body: 'Mean squared error is not what humans perceive. The ear cannot hear quiet tones next to a loud one (masking); the eye resolves color at half the resolution of brightness and barely sees high spatial frequencies at normal viewing distance. A perceptual model redefines distortion so that errors in those places count as zero, then the quantizer spends its budget where it shows. MP3\'s psychoacoustic model and JPEG\'s quantization tables are both this stage. None of it runs in the decoder.',
      demo: 'psychoacoustic', takeaway: 'Perceptual coding = change the error metric, then let the quantizer loose.' },
    { id: 'asym', title: 'Asymmetry: where the compute goes',
      body: 'Where is the compute? MP3 and video codecs run the expensive parts (psychoacoustic analysis, motion search, rate-distortion optimization) in the encoder and leave the decoder a table lookup and an inverse transform. Context mixing and neural codecs are symmetric: both ends do the same work. LZ4 is asymmetric the other way round from PAQ. The task decides: one encode and a billion decodes wants a heavy encoder; a phone call wants both light; an archive nobody reads can be heavy on both.',
      demo: 'motion', takeaway: 'Read Encoder compute against Decoder compute before you pick a single algorithm.' },
  ],
  3: [
    { id: 'sideinfo', title: 'Side information',
      body: 'The stream is half the story. The other half is what both ends agreed on in advance: the <b>prior</b>. Practitioners call it by what it is: a <b>dictionary</b> (zstd, Brotli), a <b>codebook</b> (VQ), <b>tables</b> (JPEG, MP3), a <b>model</b> or <b>checkpoint</b> (neural codecs); the textbook word is side information. A self-describing stream carries its own tables (DEFLATE dynamic blocks) and needs nothing shipped. Everything else trades bytes on the wire for bytes installed at the receiver, plus versioning: a frame header says "dictionary #7", and if you lost #7 you lost the data.',
      demo: 'dictionary', duel: 5, takeaway: 'Ask of any codec: what must the decoder already have, how big is it, and who ships it?' },
    { id: 'crossentropy', title: 'A model trained on A, used on B',
      body: 'A coder driven by model Q, on data that really follows P, pays the <b>cross-entropy</b> H(P,Q) = H(P) + D<sub>KL</sub>(P‖Q) bits per symbol. The KL term is the mismatch tax: zero when the model matches the data, large when it does not. A zstd dictionary trained on your JSON is a Q close to your P. gzip\'s fixed tables are a Q built for "typical files". A dictionary trained on English logs and used on Japanese logs still decodes correctly (the ID matches), it just saves nothing. The bits a shared prior saves are exactly the information the prior and the data have in common.',
      demo: 'cm', takeaway: 'Compression gain from a prior = how much the prior already knew about this data.' },
    { id: 'vq', title: 'Codebooks: the prior as a list of points',
      body: 'A scalar grid in d dimensions is a cube lattice; real data lives on a thin manifold inside the cube. k-means puts K code vectors where the data is, and each vector costs log<sub>2</sub> K bits. The codebook itself (K × d numbers) is side information. K grows exponentially with bits, so residual VQ stacks small codebooks: 8 × 1024 entries gives 80 bits per frame and 1024<sup>8</sup> effective points from 8192 stored vectors. Those 8 integers per frame are also the "audio tokens" that language models like AudioLM are trained on.',
      demo: 'rvq', duel: 3, takeaway: 'VQ = learn the grid from the data. RVQ = stack small grids. The codebook is shipped, not sent.' },
    { id: 'latency', title: 'Latency is a wall',
      body: 'Algorithmic delay = frame length + lookahead + transform overlap, before any network. A conversation tolerates about 150 ms mouth-to-ear in total, and the codec gets 20-30 ms of it. MP3\'s 1152-sample frames plus psychoacoustic lookahead cost over 100 ms: fine for a portable player, fatal for a call. Opus runs 2.5-20 ms frames; SoundStream uses causal convolutions so each frame depends only on the past. No amount of ratio buys you back a missed deadline.',
      demo: 'mdct', takeaway: 'Check Latency tolerance first; it eliminates whole families.' },
    { id: 'learned', title: 'Learned codecs: same stages, trained together',
      body: 'SoundStream keeps the classic stages: a transform (the encoder network), a quantizer (RVQ), a prior (the decoder network and codebooks), a perceptual model (the discriminator, training-time only). What changed is that they are optimized jointly for the final metric, and that the prior is enormous: tens of megabytes of weights that already know what speech sounds like. The 6 kbps stream is a pointer into that knowledge. That is why it beats MP3 at 1/20 of the bitrate, and why it fails on sounds it was never trained on.',
      demo: 'decoder_net', takeaway: 'Neural codec = classic pipeline + a very large, very specific prior.' },
  ],
  4: [
    { id: 'noentropy', title: 'When no entropy coder is the right entropy coder',
      body: 'Variable-length codes are sequential: to find element i you decode elements 0..i−1 or keep an index. A GPU thread dequantizing weight 7,000,000 cannot do either, and neither can an attention kernel reading token 80,000\'s key. So GGUF, KV caches and Faiss PQ use <b>fixed-length codes</b> and pay the full nominal width: 4 bits means 4 bits, even though the int4 histogram\'s entropy is maybe 3.3. The lost 10-25% buys random access and parallel decode. Research formats that entropy-code weights exist; they decompress to int4 before the kernel runs.',
      demo: 'blockint', takeaway: 'Random access + parallelism ⇒ fixed length ⇒ an empty Entropy coding stage, on purpose.' },
    { id: 'blocks', title: 'Anatomy of a quantized block',
      body: 'GGUF <b>Q4_0</b>: 32 weights → one fp16 scale (16 bits) + 32 × 4-bit integers = 144 bits, so 4.5 bits per weight; dequant is w = s·q. <b>Q8_0</b>: 16 + 32×8 = 272 bits, 8.5 bpw. <b>Q4_K_M</b>: super-blocks of 256 with 6-bit scales and minimums per 32, about 4.5 bpw with better accuracy because the mins recentre each block. Why small blocks? One outlier sets the block\'s scale, and with 32 neighbours the damage is contained. The encoder may be arbitrarily clever about <i>which</i> integer to round to (GPTQ uses second-order information, AWQ scales by activation statistics); the layout does not change.',
      demo: 'blockint', takeaway: 'bits/weight = (scale bits + n·q bits)/n. Small blocks localize outliers.' },
    { id: 'rotation', title: 'Outliers and rotations',
      body: 'Transformer keys have a few channels with 20× the magnitude of the rest. Per-block absmax quantization then wastes almost all its levels on those channels. Two fixes: quantize keys per channel (KIVI), or rotate first. An orthogonal rotation H (a Hadamard matrix, for free in O(n log n)) preserves dot products: <b>(Hq)·(Hk) = q·k</b>, so attention scores are unchanged, while the outlier\'s energy is spread evenly over all channels and the grid fits. QuaRot and SpinQuant do this to weights and activations; it is the PCA rotation from the billion-scale vector search task with a different goal: not compacting energy but flattening it.',
      demo: 'pca', duel: 4, takeaway: 'A rotation changes no information. It changes how well an axis-aligned grid fits.' },
    { id: 'ans', title: 'ANS: the entropy bound at table speed',
      body: 'Asymmetric numeral systems encode a symbol by pushing it onto a single integer state: x′ = ⌊x/f<sub>s</sub>⌉·M + cum<sub>s</sub> + (x mod f<sub>s</sub>), so log<sub>2</sub> x grows by about −log<sub>2</sub>(f<sub>s</sub>/M) per symbol, the information content. Decoding pops in reverse (LIFO). The tabled variant (tANS, zstd\'s FSE) precomputes the state machine so there are no multiplications; rANS interleaves several streams for SIMD. Result: arithmetic-coding ratio at Huffman speed, which is why every codec designed after 2014 uses it.',
      demo: 'ans', takeaway: 'ANS ended the Huffman-vs-arithmetic trade-off. Decoding runs backwards.' },
    { id: 'lmcompress', title: 'Modeling is compression',
      body: 'Any model that outputs P(next symbol | context) is a compressor once you attach an arithmetic coder: the cost is Σ −log<sub>2</sub> P(actual symbol), which is exactly the model\'s log-loss. A language model\'s perplexity in bits per character <i>is</i> a compression ratio. LLMs push English text to ~0.8 bits/char where zstd manages ~2.5, because they carry a far better prior; the catch is that the model must run bit-identically at both ends, for every symbol, which is why the Hutter Prize measures decompressor size plus output. A learned probability model is the extreme end of the Prediction stage you met on level 1.',
      demo: 'neural_prior', takeaway: 'Perplexity in bits = compressed size. A better predictor is a better compressor, full stop.' },
  ],
};

